import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The server",
	description: "The rocx server platform: Roc compiled to native code in a Rust host. Every request a call of its own that pauses while it waits, streams as loops, crashes that end one call, and memory that comes back.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "layers", label: "What is Rust, what is Roc" },
		{ id: "calls", label: "A call pauses while it waits" },
		{ id: "streams", label: "Streams" },
		{ id: "crashes", label: "A crash ends one call" },
		{ id: "memory", label: "Memory" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "Pages, rendered here" },
		{ id: "background", label: "Background loops, isolated calls" },
		{ id: "plugins", label: "Plugins" },
		{ id: "tcp", label: "Tcp and Random" },
		{ id: "running", label: "Running it" },
		{ id: "logging", label: "The log" },
		{ id: "deploying", label: "Deploying" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the server"
				title="Roc compiled to native code, in a Rust host"
			>
				One executable: the app's Roc, as machine code, linked with a host written in Rust on tokio and
				hyper. The host answers HTTP, runs each call into Roc where it may wait, catches what crashes,
				and frees what it held. Roc decides everything a request comes to; the host carries it.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="layers">What is Rust, what is Roc</Heading>
			<p>
				The host gives Roc general things: HTTP, threads, database connections, sockets, sleeping and
				waking, a crash that ends one call. What an app is built from is the platform's Roc, on top of
				those, and an app could write its own the same way:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Rust:</strong> HTTP (tokio, hyper), the database (a pool of Postgres connections and one
					that listens, or SQLite's file in the process, its calls on threads of their own), time zones,
					TCP and TLS sockets, live topics and rooms, background loops, isolated calls, actors' mailboxes
					and timers, values in memory (<code>Kv</code>), secure random numbers, rendering pages of
					components.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Roc</strong> (<code>server/platform/*.roc</code>): migrations, plugins (checked and
					run), actors' state and messages, <code>Db</code>'s decoding. Jobs and cron, and email (an SMTP
					client, MIME), are Roc too, on the same effects, in packages (<code>packages/jobs</code>,
					<code>packages/email</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="calls">A call pauses while it waits</Heading>
			<p>
				Roc runs on threads of its own, as many as the machine has cores (<code>--roc-threads</code>, or
				<code>ROCX_ROC_THREADS</code>, says otherwise), beside tokio's, which answer HTTP and drive every
				socket and timer. Each call into Roc (a request and the page it renders, a stream, an actor's turn,
				a background loop) is a coroutine on one of them, with a stack of its own: 8 MiB, Roc's default,
				reserved rather than used. When an effect waits (the database, a socket, another server, a sleep), the
				call pauses: its stack stays as it is, its thread runs other calls meanwhile, and the call resumes
				where it was, on the same thread, when what it waited on is done. So a call that waits holds no
				thread, and any number of them can wait at once. Eight requests that each wait 0.3 s on Postgres
				take about 0.3 s together, and 2,000 that each wait a second, on two threads, about a second; the
				tests check both.
			</p>
			<p>
				It has costs. Roc that computes for long without an effect (a long pure loop) can't pause, so the
				calls on its thread wait for it; new calls go to Roc's other threads. A paused call keeps its stack,
				though only the pages it touched cost memory. Profilers and debuggers see calls on stacks of their
				own rather than threads. And a call that overflows its stack ends the process, as a thread's
				overflow did, saying so on stderr first.
			</p>
			<p>
				A value Roc makes belongs to its call until the call returns; after that the host owns it and moves
				it (a stream, to the call that sends it), never sharing it. Calls nest: an isolated call (a job)
				runs inside the one that made it, with its own context; an actor's turn is a call of its own. Each
				call has a context: its transaction, the rooms it joined, the topics it listens to and its sockets,
				all given back when it ends.
			</p>

			<Heading id="streams">Streams</Heading>
			<p>
				An endpoint's events are a loop (<code>{"Events.stream(|{ send!, sleep! }| …)"}</code>), and the host
				runs it as a call of its own for as long as it is open: <code>send!</code> writes an event to the
				response, <code>sleep!</code> pauses, and wakes early if the client goes, so its next
				<code>send!</code> answers <code>Err(Gone)</code> and <code>?</code> ends the loop. A stream holds no
				thread while it sleeps: the tests keep 2,000 open on two threads, and a request beside them is
				answered at once. <a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>Events</a> shows one, and a component listening
				to it.
			</p>
			<p>
				What pages follow live is still better a <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#channels"}>channel</a>: a
				tab's channels share one stream, which the host keeps without a call, and Roc runs only for a
				channel's check or hook, a call at a time.
			</p>

			<Heading id="crashes">A crash ends one call</Heading>
			<p>
				Roc's ABI says <code>roc_crashed</code> must never return: it may end the process, or jump out of
				the Roc code that called it. The host does the second. A guard (<code>server/host/src/guard.c</code>)
				sets a jump target around every call into Roc; <code>roc_crashed</code> jumps back to it, and the
				call ends there:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>A request</strong> is answered 500, with the message under <code>--dev</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>A stream</strong> ends.</li>
				<li><strong>A job</strong> runs again later, as one that failed.</li>
				<li><strong>An actor's turn</strong> is logged and told to the asker, and the actor's state stays as it was.</li>
				<li><strong>A transaction</strong> the call had open is rolled back.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The frames a crash skips are Roc's and a small trampoline's, never the host's: no Rust destructor is
				skipped. An integer that overflows, or a <code>crash</code> in the app's code: either ends its call
				only.
			</p>

			<Heading id="memory">Memory</Heading>
			<p>
				A crash mustn't leak. While a call runs, each allocation it makes is linked into a list of its own.
				If the call returns, the answer's strings are copied out and the list freed (a stream keeps its list
				until the call that sends it); if it crashes, everything in its list is freed. Roc's reference counts
				are atomic in native builds, so a value moved to another thread stays right.
			</p>
			<p>
				The tests crash 200 requests at once, half of them after allocating 2,000 strings, and check that Roc
				holds no allocation afterwards (<code>--debug</code> serves the count at
				<code>/__rocx/allocations</code>). This site's tests render its pages again and again and check the
				same.
			</p>

			<Heading id="pages">Pages, rendered here</Heading>
			<p>
				A page the app answers is one tree of components: its layouts, the page, and what they hold. The
				host renders it whole (<code>server/host/src/signals.rs</code>), as the browser's runtime would:
				every component run once, every hole once, nothing tracked and nothing waiting, a memo computed when
				it is made, an endpoint a component reads answered by its route, in-process. The answers go in a
				script at the end of the page, each under its URL, with <code>/rocx/start.js</code>, so the browser
				reads them instead of asking. A <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> is rendered as plain HTML, with no
				markers in it: the browser never hydrates it.
			</p>
			<p>
				A failure (a context nothing provides, a memo that failed) shows the nearest
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code>'s fallback, as in the browser; a crash ends the render, and the page is
				answered with 500. A read (<code>Endpoint.get!</code>) is a GET of one of the app's own
				paths, which the app's <code>respond!</code> answers, behind the route's middleware. While the host
				renders, it is that request, made in-process (<code>Host.fetch!</code>):
				<code>accept: application/json</code>, the page's headers, cookies among them, the context the server
				started with, and the same answer the browser would get. Each URL is asked once a render, and its
				answer is noted whatever its status, unless it was read inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code>, whose
				reads the browser never makes. An answer that isn't 200 fails the read, and the nearest
				<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> shows its status and text; a redirect makes the page that redirect. The
				site's tests compare what the host renders with what the browser's
				runtime renders, byte for byte, on every page.
			</p>

			<Heading id="background">Background loops, isolated calls</Heading>
			<p>
				At start, the host asks the platform's Roc which loops to run in the background (the plugins' loops
				and checks: the jobs plugin's workers and its schedules, the email plugin's look at the SMTP
				server) and runs each, a call of its own, until the server stops. A loop sleeps with <code>Live.next!</code>, which wakes when what it listens to is published
				(<code>Db.notify!</code>, and Postgres's <code>NOTIFY</code>, are published on <code>db:channel</code>), when its time is up, or
				with <code>Gone</code> when the server stops. One that crashes is started again a second later.
			</p>
			<p>
				An isolated call runs Roc inside another call, guarded on its own: a crash in it ends it alone, and
				the caller is told, with why. A plugin's calls run that way (<code>Plugin.isolated!</code>): the
				jobs plugin's worker runs each job so, so a job that crashes is a job that failed, and the worker
				goes on. An actor's turn is a guarded call of its own, which its mailbox in
				the host runs as a call of its own with the kind, key, state and message as strings, so a crash
				frees all of them. No lock is taken anywhere: one task routes every message to its actor's mailbox,
				and each actor handles its own, one at a time.
			</p>

			<Heading id="plugins">Plugins</Heading>
			<p>
				A package can add to an app what only the server runs: routes, paths for development, files,
				scripts every page loads, tables, background loops, calls run on their own, work at start, checks.
				It gives one value, a <code>Plugin.Value</code>, made from options of its own; the app names the
				package in its header and lists the plugin in its program. A plugin's routes answer behind the app's
				middleware, with what it hands on, before the app's own routes; its tables are applied after the
				platform's and before the app's, recorded under the plugin's name.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\", acme: \"../packages/acme/main.roc\" }\n\nimport acme.Acme\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tcontext,\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\tplugins: [Acme.plugin({ at: \"/acme\" })],\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				Starting, the server checks every plugin at once, and any problem stops it, with all of them: two
				routes at one path (the app's and a plugin's, or two plugins'), a file at the root where a route is,
				two files at one place, a name used twice, an environment variable a plugin needs and doesn't have,
				a database it needs, a plugin it needs that isn't listed. Then it says on the log what each added.
				A plugin package names the server platform in its header, as an app does; what the browser can
				compile too it lists in a second header, <code>browser.roc</code>, which the browser's half sees
				instead (D-033).
			</p>
			<p>
				A plugin's files are embedded when the app is built (<code>{"import \"static/push-sw.js\" as worker : List(U8)"}</code>)
				and handed to the host once, at start. The host serves each before anything else sees the request, the
				app's middleware among it, so a guard never blocks a service worker or a script: at
				<code>{"/rocx/plugins/<plugin>/<path>"}</code>, or at <code>{"/<path>"}</code> for one <code>at: Root</code>
				(a service worker, whose scope is where it is), <code>GET</code> and <code>HEAD</code> with its type and
				an ETag of its bytes (304 when the browser has them). Its scripts, every page the server renders
				loads, a module each before start.js; a script registers the browser effects
				<code>Browser.call!</code> finds, named <code>{"<plugin>.<effect>"}</code>, and each is given
				<code>{"{ under }"}</code>, which puts a URL under the site's base (D-050).
			</p>
			<Code
				title="A plugin's files"
				source={"import \"static/push-sw.js\" as worker : List(U8)\nimport \"static/push.js\" as script : List(U8)\n\nfiles: [\n\t{ path: \"push-sw.js\", at: Root, content_type: \"text/javascript\", body: worker },\n\t{ path: \"push.js\", at: Plugin, content_type: \"text/javascript\", body: script },\n],\nscripts: [\"push.js\"],"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>

			<Heading id="tcp">Tcp and Random</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Tcp.connect!</code> (or <code>connect_tls!</code>, or <code>start_tls!</code> on an open one)
				opens a socket for the call; <code>write_bytes!</code>, <code>read_exactly!</code>,
				<code>read_up_to!</code> and <code>read_until!</code> use it, the call paused while each waits. TLS is
				rustls, checked against the web's certificate authorities. A socket the call leaves open is closed
				when it ends. <code>Random.seed_u64!</code> is the operating system's randomness. The email package's
				SMTP client is written on these, and so is <a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#roc-pg"}>roc-pg</a>, a
				Postgres client in Roc.
			</p>

			<Heading id="running">Running it</Heading>
			<Code
				title="The app's executable"
				source={"<app> --port 8080 --host 127.0.0.1        where to listen (PORT)\n      --static DIR, --file /PATH=FILE     files served as they are\n      --context-file FILE                 what respond! is given besides each request\n      --database-url URL                  the database (DATABASE_URL): postgres://…, or sqlite:<path> for a SQLite app's file elsewhere\n      --actor-idle-ms 900000              an actor idle this long is let go (0: never; kept in the database)\n      --kv-max-mb 64                      Kv's limit (KV_MAX_MB): the least recently used goes past it\n      --max-body 16MiB                    the most a request's body may be (MAX_BODY): bytes, or KiB, MiB, GiB\n      --public-url https://app.example    where the app is, for links it sends (PUBLIC_URL; else where it listens)\n      --dev                               reload pages on restart, show crashes, plugins' dev pages\n      --debug                             GET /__rocx/allocations and /__rocx/plugin-files, and plugins' debug paths\n      --live-beat-ms 15000                how often a channel stream beats, and its checks run again\n      --roc-threads N                     the threads Roc runs on (ROCX_ROC_THREADS; one a core)"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				A request's body may be 16 MiB at most (413 beyond), or what <code>--max-body</code>
				(<code>MAX_BODY</code>) says: a number of bytes, or of KiB, MiB or GiB (<code>64MiB</code>). The
				host reads a body whole before Roc runs, so it is the most a request holds in memory. Requests to
				other servers go through <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> and <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> when they are set,
				but not to a host <code>NO_PROXY</code> names (<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#connections"}>HTTP</a>).
				Starting, the server takes its port first: one
				in use stops it there, before the database, the migrations or the background loops start, so
				nothing is left running. Stopping (SIGTERM, or ^C), the server stops
				listening and taking jobs, and waits up to ten seconds for the requests, streams and jobs still
				running. Without a database, what needs one says so when it is used; the rest works.
			</p>

			<Heading id="logging">The log</Heading>
			<p>
				Every line the server writes is an event: a level, a message and fields. The host's own lines
				(starting, stopping, a crash with its method, target and error, forwarding) and the app's go through
				one formatter, in the host, and so do the platform's and its plugins' (a migration applied, what a
				plugin added, a job that failed, mail kept): a message that doesn't change, the rest in fields
				(<code>rocx jobs: a job failed, and runs again</code>, a warning with the job's <code>kind</code>,
				<code>id</code>, <code>attempt</code>, <code>again_in_ms</code> and <code>error</code>), so a
				collected log filters on them. An app logs with <code>Log.info!</code>, and <code>debug!</code>,
				<code>warn!</code> and <code>error!</code>, its fields a record written as JSON;
				<code>Log.line!</code> is a message alone. The format is the app's to choose, in its program:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An event, and the log's format"
				source={"Log.info!(\"gate failed\", { path: request.path, error: why })\n\nprogram = {\n\t..App.program({ context, middleware }),\n\tlogs: { format: Auto }, # or Pretty, or Json\n}"}
			/>
			<Code
				title="The line, pretty and as JSON"
				source={"14:03:12 INFO  gate failed error=\"no session\" path=/admin\n{\"time\":\"2026-11-21T13:03:12.345Z\",\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"gate failed\",\"error\":\"no session\",\"path\":\"/admin\"}"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Auto</code>, which <code>App.program</code> says, is <code>ROCX_LOG</code>
				(<code>pretty</code> or <code>json</code>) if it is set, or else pretty when stderr is a terminal,
				the level in colour, and JSON when it isn't: a server whose log is collected. A field can't take
				the place of <code>time</code>, <code>level</code> or <code>msg</code> (it is written
				<code>fields.msg</code>). Logging doesn't wait for stderr: the event goes on a queue, and a thread of
				its own writes the lines in order, flushing when the queue is empty, and before the process exits. A
				full queue (64k events) makes the caller wait rather than lose a line.
			</p>

			<Heading id="deploying">Deploying</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>node server/build.mjs app.roc out/app --target=arm64musl</code> (or <code>x64musl</code>)
				cross-compiles a static Linux executable from macOS: Rust's own musl target supplies the C library and
				the unwinder, and Zig compiles the host's C. Its migrations are in it; copy it into a container with
				the app's static files, give it a <code>DATABASE_URL</code> (a Postgres app's; a SQLite app's file goes
				on a volume, <code>sqlite:/data/app.db</code>), and put a proxy in front for TLS, telling
				the app where that is with <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> (<code>Env.public_url!</code>, for the links it sends). The platform's test
				app, built that way, runs every one of its tests in a container against <code>just db</code>'s
				Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>HTTP/1.1, no TLS:</strong> put it behind a proxy.</li>
				<li><strong>A stack overflow in Roc</strong> still ends the process (it says so first).</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Built on">
				<p>
					tokio and hyper (HTTP), tokio-postgres with deadpool, rusqlite with SQLite compiled in, chrono-tz
					(time zones), rustls with ring (TLS, and random numbers),
					and Roc's glue for the types the header names (<code>just glue</code> writes
					<code>server/host/src/roc_platform_abi.rs</code>).
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
